# Assignment 2.3 — Div, Attributes & Clean Structure

## Objective
To organize a personal introduction webpage into structured sections using `div` containers, unique `id` attributes, reusable `class` attributes, and custom `data-*` attributes.

## Solution

First, I opened the previous personal introduction webpage in Visual Studio Code. I updated the HTML code to make it clean, readable, and well-structured.

I divided the webpage into five main sections: Header, About, Education, Skills, and Contact. I used `div` elements to group related content into separate containers.

I assigned unique `id` attributes to the main sections so that each section could be identified individually. I also used reusable `class` attributes to apply common styles to multiple elements. Additionally, I added a custom `data-*` attribute to store extra information in an HTML element.

I updated the text in the About section and added new content to the Education, Skills, and Contact sections. Finally, I maintained proper indentation and used meaningful names for IDs and classes to make the code easy to understand and maintain.

## HTML Code

Save the following code as `index.html`.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Personal Introduction</title>
</head>
<body>

    <!-- Header Section -->
    <div id="header" class="section">
        <h1>Your Name</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Student and Aspiring Web Developer</p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- About Section -->
    <div id="about" class="section">
        <h2>About Me</h2>
        <p>
            Hello! My name is Your Name. I am a student interested
            in web development and programming. I enjoy learning
            new technologies and developing my technical skills.
        </p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- Education Section -->
    <div id="education" class="section">
        <h2>Education</h2>
        <div class="content-box">
            <h3>My Academic Background</h3>
            <p>Qualification: Your Current Qualification</p>
            <p>Institution: Your School or College Name</p>
            <p>Year: 2026</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- Skills Section -->
    <div id="skills" class="section">
        <h2>My Skills</h2>
        <div class="content-box">
            <p class="skill-item">HTML - Creating webpage structure</p>
            <p class="skill-item">CSS - Styling webpages</p>
            <p class="skill-item">JavaScript - Adding interactivity</p>
            <p class="skill-item">Communication and teamwork</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- Contact Section -->
    <div id="contact" class="section"
         data-section-type="contact-information">
        <h2>Contact Me</h2>
        <p>Email: your-email@example.com</p>
        <p>Phone: Your Phone Number</p>
        <p>Location: Your City</p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- Footer Section -->
    <div id="footer" class="section">
        <p>&copy; 2026 Your Name. All rights reserved.</p>
    </div>

</body>
</html>
```

**Note:** Replace `Your Name`, your email address, phone number, institution name, qualification, and city with your own details.

## Explanation of HTML Attributes

1. **`id` attribute:** Gives a unique name to an element, such as `id="about"` or `id="skills"`.
2. **`class` attribute:** Groups elements that can share the same styling or behavior, such as `class="section"` and `class="content-box"`.
3. **`data-*` attribute:** Stores custom information on an HTML element. In this assignment, `data-section-type="contact-information"` identifies the purpose of the Contact section.
4. **`div` element:** Groups related HTML elements into separate containers.
5. **HTML comments:** Describe each section and make the code easier to understand.

## Result

The personal introduction webpage was successfully reorganized into five main sections: Header, About, Education, Skills, and Contact. Unique IDs, reusable classes, and a custom data attribute were added. The text was updated, new content was included, and the HTML code was organized with proper indentation.

